{:receipt :kotobase/fold-cost-at-production-shape
 :date "2026-08-17"
 :question
 "The background fold deployed today (net-kotobase/control-plane merge 8f6955eab7e,
  Cloudflare version 26059ea8, protocols-worker, threshold 8 via ctx.waitUntil) is
  DORMANT -- it fires only after a commit and nothing has written to
  kotobase-protocols-v2 since. When it fires it will fold that graph. Does that
  fold fit a Cloudflare Worker's CPU budget, and with how much margin?

  The failure mode being priced: a fold that exceeds the budget dies AFTER the
  response was already sent, so nothing surfaces it. Novelty never drops, every
  subsequent write schedules another fold, every fold dies, and the graph reads
  exactly like a graph nobody has written to. `fold!`'s own docstring names this
  runaway. It is the silence-that-reads-as-success class."

 :verdict
 {:answer :fits
  :fold-cpu-ms {:median 4102 :range [3806 5142] :n 8
                :at-the-exact-production-shape [3828 3839]}
  :budget-ms 30000
  :margin "~7.3x at the fully-real production point (3.83s of 30s = 12.8% of budget).
           Worst run of eight was 5.14s = 17% of budget, still ~5.8x."
  :independently-corroborated
  "A fold with this shape ALREADY COMPLETED in production. Chain seq 908 -> 909 took
   novelty-count 64 -> 0 and wrote a fresh `indexed` snapshot; no fold has run since,
   so the snapshot this receipt measured (bafyreigogf36uo... , 4,215 quads / 11.4 MB)
   IS the one that fold produced, inside the Worker, inside the budget. Measured by a
   parallel agent, 90-docs/kotobase-performance/2026-08-17-novelty-chain-node-shapes.edn
   (merge 2dc6c4ce60b). This receipt's benchmark and that production observation agree."
  :bounding-not-needed-here
  "`max-novelty` (eng/fold!'s 4th positional, currently nil = unbounded) would not
   help THIS graph. Measured: folding 63 unfolded transactions costs the same as
   folding 8 (3.83s vs 3.81s -- inside run-to-run noise). The novelty half of this
   graph is 77,864 bytes of ciphertext across 63 blocks; the snapshot rebuild is
   34.6 MB. Bounding caps the cheap half. See :why-this-graph-differs-from-relay-bsky."}

