 ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------ method
 :method
 {:what "A benchmark, not a unit test: test/kotobase_protocols_worker/fold_bench.cljs
         in net-kotobase/control-plane, plus a `:bench` :node-script build. Kept out
         of `npm test` (the suite's :ns-regexp is \"-test$\"); the suite is unchanged
         at 102 tests / 694 assertions / 0 failures with this added."
  :fidelity
  {:snapshot "REAL. The 21 spo blocks were fetched from production R2, decoded back to
              [s p o] triples the way cold-datoms does, and re-committed with
              arrangement.core/commit!."
   :self-check "The rebuilt snapshot CID is
                bafyreigogf36uo5jrah2pzmggojnghadmhzjl3z7xwc3mfxzbfnpzkt4ye -- byte-
                identical to the one production's head points at. If it had not
                matched, no number in this receipt would mean anything, which is why
                it is checked rather than assumed."
   :novelty "REAL for the headline point (:label prod-REAL-fold): all 63 production
             tx blocks fetched, their quads decoded out of the `ct` slot and replayed
             through eng/commit! so the queue's node shape is what push-novelty!
             actually writes (4 segments, matching production). Earlier rows used
             synthetic novelty; both are reported and they agree, which is itself the
             evidence that novelty size is not the cost driver on this graph."
   :code "The shipped `kotobase-store/fold-now!` itself, no substitutions, compiled
          with `shadow-cljs release` -- the same :advanced optimization level the
          deployed Worker is built with. A dev-build run of the same case cost 6.72s
          vs 4.49s release, so measuring at :none would have overstated CPU by ~50%."}

  :units
  {:primary "process.cpuUsage() delta, user+system microseconds. Chosen because (a) it
             is the exact quantity Cloudflare limits -- error 1102 / `Worker exceeded
             CPU time limit` is a CPU-time trip, not wall-clock -- and (b) it is
             load-robust: this workstation ran many concurrent agent sessions all day
             and the 1-minute load average during these runs ranged 9.9 to 28.0.
             Across that 2.8x load range the same case measured 3.83s to 4.60s, and
             the two runs at the HIGHEST load (27-28) were the FASTEST (3.83s),
             which is the signature of a load-independent metric."
   :structural "Operation counts from an instrumented in-memory R2 binding -- the same
                fake-bucket shape kotobase_store_test.cljs uses, plus counters. Counts
                are properties of the graph shape and the code, so they reproduce on a
                quiet host. Same reason kotobase-peer's own dag_shape_bench counts.
                `:gets` counts CALLS and `:distinct-get-keys` counts KEYS: the two
                differ exactly when the code fetches the same bytes twice, which
                turned out to matter -- see :secondary-finding."
   :wall-clock "Reported, but only ever beside the load average that produced it."}

  :mirror-validated
  "`fold-mirror!` is an instrumented copy of fold-now!'s wiring, needed because the
   shipped path exposes no seam for counting trampoline restarts. A drifted mirror
   measures nothing, so it is checked against the shipped call: same resulting chain
   CID, same R2 GET/PUT counts and bytes, on both the 63- and 8-novelty cases.
   63: chain bafyreid2r6ikhhg35b4m747dr2ylxobg3bbtyp4zvigtcknviuf6v3xu3i,
       gets 91 / 11,400,441 B, puts 54 / 34,217,102 B -- identical in both modes.
   8:  chain bafyreihlyo34cjxzo7iqywfp2mowaukd2jdt6slfdufwy5yvvwcxs7usfi,
       gets 33 / 11,387,862 B, puts 52 / 34,156,499 B -- identical in both modes."}

