 :novelty-is-not-the-driver-on-this-graph
 {:evidence "63 unfolded transactions cost 3,806-4,600 ms; 8 cost 3,806-5,142 ms. The
             two ranges overlap almost completely, and the fully-real 63-transaction
             point (3,828/3,839 ms) is at the BOTTOM of both. 55 extra transactions
             are worth less than the run-to-run spread."
  :why "This graph's novelty is 77,864 bytes of ciphertext total. The snapshot
        rebuild that runs regardless is 34.6 MB. The novelty half is 0.22% of the
        bytes the fold touches."}

 :cost-model
 {:claim "Fold CPU is linear in the BYTES of snapshot rewritten, not in quad count
          and not in unfolded-transaction count."
  :curve "Synthetic snapshots, novelty 8, release build, one process per case."
  :rows
  [{:label "synth-500-n8"  :snapshot-quads 500  :put-bytes 10770852  :cpu-ms 1369.3  :ms-per-mb 127}
   {:label "synth-1000-n8" :snapshot-quads 1000 :put-bytes 21774207  :cpu-ms 2450.6  :ms-per-mb 113}
   {:label "synth-2000-n8" :snapshot-quads 2000 :put-bytes 45705617  :cpu-ms 5353.4  :ms-per-mb 117}
   {:label "synth-4000-n8" :snapshot-quads 4000 :put-bytes 93572676  :cpu-ms 12359.9 :ms-per-mb 132}
   {:label "synth-8000-n8" :snapshot-quads 8000 :put-bytes 189354938 :cpu-ms 29327.9 :ms-per-mb 155}
   {:label "production"    :snapshot-quads 4215 :put-bytes 34625780  :cpu-ms 3838.8  :ms-per-mb 114}]
  :fit "115-155 ms of CPU per MB of snapshot rewritten, mildly superlinear at the top
        (GC pressure). Quad count alone does NOT predict cost: the synthetic 4,000-quad
        case cost 12.4s and the real 4,215-quad case cost 3.8s, because the synthetic
        values are ~3x heavier. Bytes predict cost; quads do not."
  :ceiling "At 115-155 ms/MB a 30,000 ms budget is exhausted somewhere around
            190-260 MB rewritten. The synth-8000 row is the empirical anchor: 189 MB
            cost 29.3s, i.e. it landed ON the limit. Production rewrites 34.6 MB, so
            this graph can grow roughly 5.5x-7.5x in BYTES before a fold stops fitting."
  :what-rewriting-means "A fold rewrites every non-empty index in full. Here that is
                         three trees (ocp is empty) over the same 4,215 quads, which
                         is why 11.4 MB read becomes 34.6 MB written."}

 ;; -------------------------------------------------------- secondary finding
 :secondary-finding
 {:title "The READ path fetches the whole snapshot up to three times per request. The
          fold path does not. One line of evaluation order separates them."
  :status :measured-both-directions
  :why-it-was-looked-for
  "The live handler cost (4.0-7.1s, 2026-08-17-live-latency receipt) had been
   attributed to a 21-hop novelty walk. The walk is 4 hops, so that arithmetic was
   void and the time had to be somewhere else. The two named suspects were per-entry
   read-tx-block and a >=21-block snapshot scan."

  :measured
  {:harness "`--op hydrate` runs hydrate-run-persist!'s read half verbatim: head read,
             then ks/hydrate! over the same with-blocks trampoline with the same
             buffer-first async-get-fn."
   :rows
   [{:novelty 0  :gets 18  :distinct 18 :duplicate 0  :get-bytes 11381314 :cpu-ms 889.2}
    {:novelty 8  :gets 42  :distinct 27 :duplicate 15 :get-bytes 22764132 :cpu-ms 1090.0}
    {:novelty 63 :gets 114 :distinct 85 :duplicate 29 :get-bytes 34153888 :cpu-ms 1445.3}]
   :reading "At the production shape a single read transfers 34.15 MB out of R2 while
             only 11.4 MB of distinct blocks exist. 3.00x amplification. The dose
             responds to the number of NOVELTY NODES, not entries: 0 nodes -> 1.00x,
             1 node -> 2.00x, 4 nodes -> 3.00x."}

  :mechanism
  "`kotobase-peer.core/hot-datoms` builds `(js/Promise.all #js [(cold-datoms-async ..)
   (pmap-async .. (novelty-cids get-fn state))])`. Array elements evaluate left to
   right, so the snapshot scan is STARTED first; then `novelty-cids` -- which uses the
   SYNCHRONOUS get-fn -- throws a block-miss. `r2/with-blocks` catches it, fetches one
   block, and re-runs the whole body. The abandoned scan is not cancelled: it keeps
   fetching, and `cached-block-bytes` only populates its memory cache when a fetch
   RESOLVES, so the restarted copy re-requests blocks the orphan has in flight.
   `fold!` is immune because it computes `state-at` and `novelty-cids` in its `let`,
   before any async work exists -- which is exactly why every fold row above reports
   `duplicate-gets 0` and every hydrate row with novelty does not."

  :confirmed-by-experiment
  {:change "Hoisted `(novelty-cids get-fn state)` into hot-datoms' `let`, above the
            Promise.all. Local build-tree edit for measurement only; REVERTED after,
            not landed, and not proposed here as a change to an upstream repo this
            task owns."
   :before-after
   [{:novelty 8  :gets [42 27]  :get-bytes [22764132 11383165] :duplicate-gets [15 0]}
    {:novelty 63 :gets [114 85] :get-bytes [34153888 11395744] :duplicate-gets [29 0]}]
   :effect "3.00x fewer bytes at the production shape, 2.00x at 8. Duplicate gets to
            zero in both directions."
   :cpu-unchanged "1,445 -> 1,364 ms. Expected: the duplicated work is I/O, and the
                   in-memory fake bucket charges nothing for transfer. The saving is
                   real R2->Worker bytes and R2 Class B operations, not CPU. That is
                   also why this is reported as a finding and not as a fix to the CPU
                   question -- it does not move the verdict above."}

  :where-the-live-4-to-7s-goes
  {:accounted "34.15 MB transferred from R2 per read (3x more than necessary) plus
               ~1.4s of CPU. R2-to-Worker transfer of 34 MB plus 1.4s CPU is
               consistent with the observed 4.0-4.4s handler cost without needing any
               contribution from the novelty node walk."
   :not-measured "The transfer time itself. This benchmark's bucket is in-memory, so
                  it charges zero for bytes. The attribution above is arithmetic over
                  a measured byte count, not a measured transfer."
   :also-true "63 separate read-tx-block fetches DO happen (visible as 63 of the 85
               distinct keys), batched 24 at a time by pmap-async = 3 sequential
               waves. On this graph they are 77.9 KB total and cost ~0.1s, so they are
               a round-trip cost, not a byte cost. On relay-bsky they are 409 KB and
               they dominate. Same code, opposite conclusion, because the graphs
               differ -- which is the point of :why-this-graph-differs."}}

 :why-this-graph-differs-from-relay-bsky
 {:relay-bsky "ADR-2608170100: outcome exceededCpu, 32,500 ms, on graph relay-bsky via
               kotobase-cf-wasm-staging. Snapshot ~14,500 datoms worth 2.7s; the other
               29.3s is 15 unfolded novelty blocks carrying 409,250 bytes of
               ciphertext -- ~2s per entry."
  :kotobase-protocols-v2 "Snapshot 4,215 quads worth 11.4 MB and 3.8s; novelty 63
                          blocks carrying 77,864 bytes -- 5.3x LESS novelty ciphertext
                          spread over 4.2x MORE blocks."
  :consequence "The two graphs fail in opposite halves. On relay-bsky the novelty half
                is 92% of the cost and `max-novelty` is the right remedy. Here the
                novelty half is 0.22% of the bytes and `max-novelty` would cap the
                cheap half while leaving the 34.6 MB snapshot rebuild untouched --
                measured directly: folding 8 costs the same as folding 63.
                A graph-blind 'always bound the fold' rule would be inert here."
  :shared-hazard "Both call eng/fold! with max-novelty nil. protocols-worker's call is
                  `(eng/fold! put! get-fn chain ipld/link? nil ...)` in
                  kotobase_store.cljs/fold-now!. That is the same unbounded shape.
                  It is safe here today because of the byte profile above, not because
                  of anything in the call."}

 ;; ------------------------------------------------------- the threshold cost
 :cost-of-the-threshold-change
 {:what "The deploy lowered the effective threshold from 64 (inline fold-if-due!,
         retained as the no-ExecutionContext backstop) to 8 (background fold-now!)."
  :measured "A fold costs the same whether it consumes 8 or 63 transactions, because
             the cost is the snapshot rebuild. So folding 8x more often costs 8x more."
  :amortized-per-write
  [{:threshold 64 :cpu-ms-per-write 60  :r2-put-bytes-per-write 541028}
   {:threshold 8  :cpu-ms-per-write 480 :r2-put-bytes-per-write 4328223}]
  :derivation "3,839 ms and 34,625,780 bytes per fold, divided by the number of writes
               between folds."
  :what-it-buys "Reads stay near the unfolded floor instead of climbing. Measured on
                 the read path: novelty 0 costs 11.38 MB / 889 ms CPU; novelty 63
                 costs 34.15 MB / 1,445 ms. Folding at 8 keeps a read near the former."
  :not-adjudicated "Whether 8 is the right number is a product trade between read
                    latency and write-side CPU plus R2 Class A operations and storage
                    churn. This receipt supplies both sides of it and picks neither.
                    Note the numbers move together with the read-amplification finding
                    above: fixing that lowers the cost of NOT folding, which lowers the
                    value of folding early."}

 ;; ------------------------------------------------------------ what is unshown
 :what-this-does-not-show
 ["Node is not workerd. Every CPU number here is process.cpuUsage() under Node v26 on
   Darwin/arm64, running the same :advanced-optimized ClojureScript the Worker ships.
   Same V8 family, but the ratio between the two runtimes was NOT measured. The
   verdict leans on a ~7x margin precisely because that ratio is unknown; it would not
   survive a 7x runtime penalty, and nothing here rules one out. What does bound it is
   the production observation that a fold of this snapshot already completed."

  "MEMORY IS NOT ANSWERED. A Workers isolate has a 128 MB limit and this benchmark
   cannot map onto it. Node reported heapUsed 277-460 MB and RSS 533-700 MB after the
   fold, but that figure includes the benchmark's own seeded fake bucket (34 MB of
   blocks held live), the decoded triple vector, and garbage not collected since the
   single gc() before the run. It is an upper bound on a different runtime's
   accounting, not an estimate. What IS known: the fold buffers every output block in
   memory before flushing (34.6 MB across 59 blocks) and holds the 11.4 MB input plus
   a 4,215-quad four-index db at the same time. If this deploy fails in production,
   memory is the more likely cause than CPU, and this receipt did not measure it. The
   measurement that would settle it is `wrangler tail` on a real fold, reading the
   outcome field -- exceededMemory and exceededCpu are distinguishable there."

  "Whether ctx.waitUntil work is charged the same 30s CPU budget as the request that
   scheduled it was ASSUMED, not verified in this session. If waitUntil has a separate
   or smaller allowance the margin changes. The 30,000 ms figure itself is well
   supported -- protocols-worker's wrangler.jsonc declares no `limits` block, and a
   sibling Worker with no `limits` block was observed tripping at 32,500 ms
   (ADR-2608170100) -- but that observation is about a request, not a waitUntil task."

  "R2 transfer time. The benchmark's bucket is in-memory; it charges nothing for bytes
   and nothing for latency. Every byte count here is real; every inference from a byte
   count to seconds of wall time is arithmetic, not measurement."

  "Concurrency. One fold at a time, no competing request in the isolate, no CAS
   contention. fold-now! has no retry loop by design, so a lost CAS is cheap, but a
   fold running alongside a burst of reads in the same isolate was not measured."

  "Growth over time. The 5.5-7.5x byte headroom assumes the graph keeps its current
   value-size profile. It holds git object bodies inline; one large push moves the
   snapshot bytes much faster than it moves the quad count, and quad count is what
   novelty-size and every existing dashboard reports."

  "The synthetic curve is a curve in BYTES. Its quad counts are not comparable to
   production's, because its generated values are ~3x heavier per quad. Read the
   :put-bytes column, not the :snapshot-quads column."]

 :what-would-discriminate
 [{:question "Does the fold actually complete in the deployed Worker, and how close to
              the limit?"
   :test "`wrangler tail kotobase-protocols-worker` while a write lands on
          kotobase-protocols-v2, then read the cpuTime and outcome fields of the
          waitUntil task. This is the one measurement that settles CPU, memory, the
          Node-vs-workerd ratio and the waitUntil budget question at once, and it
          needs a single write the operator can authorize."
   :blocked-by "Writing requires the WRITE_TOKEN; the operator copy at the documented
                identifier did not authenticate as of today (see the 2026-08-17
                live-latency receipt). Not retried here -- guessing credential
                variants against a live auth endpoint is not debugging."}
  {:question "Is the 3x read amplification real in production, not just in this
              harness?"
   :test "Same tail, on any read: count the R2 GETs the Worker issues for one
          /health. The prediction is explicit -- ~114 GETs and ~34 MB with 63 unfolded
          transactions, falling to ~85 and ~11.4 MB once novelty is folded, and
          falling to ~85/~11.4 MB WITHOUT folding if hot-datoms' evaluation order is
          fixed."}
  {:question "Where is the real memory ceiling?"
   :test "Run the fold against progressively larger snapshots in workerd (`wrangler
          dev`) rather than Node, and find the size at which outcome becomes
          exceededMemory. The CPU curve above says CPU trips near 190-260 MB
          rewritten; if memory trips first, the ceiling is lower than this receipt's
          headroom claim."}]

